6045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428471 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428472 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1031 "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1033 1939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1034 0.9337212812567085 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "26b9f4f0-7964-52b3-9a23-d31ae6927cb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122474 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122474 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122474 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186447343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122474 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cbade43c-aee7-5def-a760-a6555c7e377c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123050 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123050 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123050 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123050 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123050 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123050 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123050 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123050 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186447333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123050 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "912b4db0-e052-5337-99c9-aff6e5410f1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124258 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3362" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124258 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y) = 6x^2+2y^2 - 2xy^2,$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (3,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124258 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a01560be-9023-593a-87d3-7412f3b208a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124258 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y) = k$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0 \\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (3,3)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(12x-2{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(4y-4xy)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇\\,f(3,3) & =(12(3)-2(3)^{2})i+(4(3)-4(3)(3))j \\\\  & =18\\,i-24\\,j \\\\  & =⟨18,-24⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the level curve that passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)=(3,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(3,3)\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨18,-24⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can leave our answer in this form. Or, if we want to leave our final answer in Cartesian form, we expand the dot product and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18(x-3)-24(y-3) & =0 \\\\ 18x-24y+18 & =0 \\\\ 3x-4y+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/10/102f55fe0add794d3ee0f9f044b5e35c4403d4f160fe36e97d737d33ef3abb68.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124259 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3363" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124259 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $x^2-3xy+4y=2.$ Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124259 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f6400ab7-3684-5f0d-b8f0-1c85b0b322ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124259 #edb/ref 1074 "First, if we set $f(x,y) = x^2-3xy+4y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1) = f_y(1,1) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(1,1) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(-3x+4)\\text{ }i-(2x-3y)\\text{ }j \\\\ t(1,1) & =(-3(1)+4)\\text{ }i-(2(1)-3(1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =1\\,i-(-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf i +\\mathbf j,$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/01/0114bf524d085402bd27178d687a5faf176b7147c8e8726b84042b17823b6b0b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124260 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3364" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124260 #edb/ref 1071 "At which points on the curve $6xy+3y^2 =7+ y - 3x^2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{j}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124260 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4ca2e8a-5a5b-54c6-983d-2f5e821084ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124260 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = 6xy+3y^2 - y + 3x^2 ,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=7.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x,y) = f_y(x,y) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x,y) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6x+6y-1)\\,i-(6y+6x)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j},$ we must have $\\mathbf t \\cdot \\mathbf j = 0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((6x+6y-1)\\,i-(6y+6x)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -6y-6x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6x(-x)+3(-x)^{2} & =7+(-x)-3{x}^{2} \\\\ -6{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & =7-x-3{x}^{2} \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if $x=7,$ we get $y=-7$ and the corresponding point is $(7,-7).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124454 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124454 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124454 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124454 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124454 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124454 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124454 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124454 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124454 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124454 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124454 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124454 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124454 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124455 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124455 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124455 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124456 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124732 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124732 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124732 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124732 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124732 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124732 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124732 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124732 #edb/ref 1045 "Tangent Lines to Level Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124732 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124732 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124732 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124732 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b218a927-38fe-5672-a4d0-f4c57476e22f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124979 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124979 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36383" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124979 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124979 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}-y$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124979 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ecf0fad-2e50-567b-a7b0-a4806d471317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124979 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(6x+2{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(4xy-1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇\\,f(-1,1) & =(6(-1)+2(1)^{2})\\,i+(4(-1)(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-4\\,i-5\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-4,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-4,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-1⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125075 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125075 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125075 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125075 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})-3{y}^{3}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\sqrt{\\pi },2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125075 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6369bf45-c149-5591-b6da-b79b10114d99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125075 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(\\sqrt{\\pi },2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})-3{y}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})-3{y}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i-9{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(\\sqrt{\\pi },2) & =-2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡((\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2})i-9(2)^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot 0\\,i-36\\,j \\\\  & =-36\\,j \\\\  & =⟨0,-36⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(\\sqrt{\\pi },2)\\cdot ⟨x-\\sqrt{\\pi },\\,y-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,-36⟩\\cdot ⟨x-\\sqrt{\\pi },\\,y-2⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125292 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125292 #edb/ref 1061 11184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125292 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7cc9655e-83bb-5f85-8c20-add1d27fb5e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125292 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125293 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125293 #edb/ref 1061 11185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125293 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "204027bd-d606-5131-82eb-717c2823cc14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125293 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125294 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125294 #edb/ref 1061 11188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125294 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6501f944-f162-5a6e-9f8e-4ccc2cd2ce41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125295 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125295 #edb/ref 1061 11189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125295 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1ce011ea-75f6-5023-9ba5-8deb530c69ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125295 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125296 #edb/ref 1061 11192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125296 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6e185929-7cee-53dd-9616-e86a123783e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125296 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125464 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125464 #edb/ref 1061 22698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125464 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e3c389c-2b5c-55ca-92a8-e52fcbcf9f84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125464 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125733 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find the equation of the **tangent line** to the ellipse \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{12} + \\dfrac{y^2}{4} =1\n$$\n\n at the point $P(3,1).$\n\nTo solve this problem, we could find the slope of the line using techniques from single-variable calculus. However, we can also solve the problem by computing the gradient of a two-variable function.\n\nLet's start by defining \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}{12} + \\dfrac {y^2}{4}.\n$$\n\nThen, the given ellipse is the level curve  $f(x,y)=1.$\n\nNow, we know that $\\nabla \\! f(3,1)$ is a vector that's normal to the level curve $f(x,y)= 1$ at $P(3,1).$ So, it's also normal to the tangent line to our ellipse at $P.$\n\n![](images/3d/3dc75e8fd4083af1bf90f46b2e875d5d552bffdc5c1cfaeabebef70f79f00828.png)\n\nSuppose that $(x,y)$ is any point on that tangent line. Then $\\mathbf v=\\langle x - 3, y-1 \\rangle$ is a vector that's parallel to the tangent line. So $\\mathbf v$ must be also orthogonal to $\\nabla f(3,1),$ and since the dot product of two orthogonal vectors is zero, we have that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(3,1)\\,\\cdot \\,\\langle x - 3, y-1 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, computing $\\nabla\\! f$ and evaluating it at the point $P(3,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{x}{6},\\frac{y}{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,f(3,1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the equation of our tangent, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(3,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x-3)+\\frac{1}{2}(y-1) & =0 \\\\ (x-3)+(y-1) & =0 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125733 #edb/ref 1065 2496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125733 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7a9dede8-0672-56eb-9084-cf581e3fb05f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125733 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125734 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a level curve $f(x,y) = f(x_0,y_0),$ a **tangent vector** is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x_0,y_0) = f_y(x_0,y_0) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x_0,y_0) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo understand why the above vector is tangent to the level curve, first recall that the gradient vector $\\nabla f(x_0,y_0)$ is normal to the level curve. Now, by computing the dot product, we see that the vector $\\mathbf t$ is orthogonal to the gradient vector $\\nabla\\! f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f\\cdot t & =({f}_{x}\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}\\text{ }j)\\cdot ({f}_{y}\\text{ }i-{f}_{x}j) \\\\  & ={f}_{x}{f}_{y}-{f}_{y}{f}_{x} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf t$ is tangent to the level curve." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125734 #edb/ref 1065 2497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125734 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6ef07808-783a-543e-9217-5d3d0a9ae13a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125734 #edb/ref 1067 "The Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125807 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, let's state the previous result more generally.\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be a function that's continuously differentiable at the point $P(x_0,y_0).$   If  $\\nabla f(x_0, y_0)$ is not the zero vector, then it is orthogonal to the tangent line to the level curve \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= f(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nat the point $P.$ The equation of the tangent can then be written as\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\! f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0,y-y_0\\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nWriting this explicitly, we get\n\n$$\nf_x(x_0,y_0)(x-x_0) + f_y(x_0,y_0)(y-y_0)   = 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125807 #edb/ref 1065 3866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125807 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e9c2ee0b-08cf-5f42-bf09-f80dcd82051d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125807 #edb/ref 1067 "Tangent Line to a Level Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263102 #edb/ref 1080 "i-j" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263102 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263102 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ab7ecfb-6837-5f43-a7dc-5de26787b651" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263311 #edb/ref 1080 "-2\\,i+j" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263311 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263311 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "23c6bb01-4817-5934-b31c-f94122a313f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263672 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+j" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263672 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263672 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4d97af51-08fe-5c7f-90df-c14d7845e35f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264290 #edb/ref 1080 "-i-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264290 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264290 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "96b21983-40a1-5c92-8a77-84386b07a32d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265047 #edb/ref 1080 "i+2\\,j" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265047 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265047 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eae56032-740f-556c-b1d0-0bf3c280086c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265481 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265481 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265481 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265481 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265481 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186265047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265481 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265481 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265481 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186263102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265481 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "86808a14-0099-527e-bba9-8dfc049cb102" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265852 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265852 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130198" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265852 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265852 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265852 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e97b736-408e-55e1-a895-d40d15156508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265852 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)={f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)i-{f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)j \\\\  & =(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)j \\\\ t(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))j \\\\  & =i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336423 #edb/ref 1080 "5\\,i-5\\,j" #edb