f 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339107 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2xy+9.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339107 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "148b9435-dbdc-5e2a-8580-1b7906c6cec6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339107 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2xy+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,2)={f}_{y}(-1,2)\\,i-{f}_{x}(-1,2)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-2x)\\,i-(2x-2y)\\,j \\\\ t(-1,2) & =(2(2)-2(-1))\\,i-(2(-1)-2(2))\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $6\\,i+6\\,j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339122 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339122 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339122 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339122 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy=1-3x.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339122 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c4a11cf-62cb-5e11-869c-4992e2a1b6f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339122 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy & =1-3x \\\\ 2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,1)={f}_{y}(1,1)i-{f}_{x}(1,1)j\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x)j \\\\  & =(4y{x}^{3}-4x)i-(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4y+3)j \\\\ t(1,1) & =(4(1)(1)^{3}-4(1))i-(6(1)^{2}(1)^{2}-4(1)+3)j \\\\  & =-5\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-5\\,j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373743 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373743 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130192" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373743 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373743 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}y={y}^{2}+3.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373743 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "591300c7-13a2-5dd3-a27a-1d6a5e36e2a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373743 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & ={y}^{2}+3 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-{y}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-{y}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,1)={f}_{y}(2,1)\\,i-{f}_{x}(2,1)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-{y}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2y)\\,i-2xy\\,j \\\\ t(2,1) & =({2}^{2}-2(1))\\,i-2(2)(1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i-4\\,j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373748 #edb/ref 17